From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Craig Johnson <1545411+captainpacket@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Tue, 8 Sep 2026 05:00:00 -0500
Subject: [PATCH] catalystwan: DeviceModel is an enum, so every model was refused

Three modules build their `device_model` choices with

    choices=list(get_args(DeviceModel))

which assumes DeviceModel is a typing.Literal. In the catalystwan we
vendor (0.41.5.dev2) it is an ENUM -- enum.EnumType, 293 members -- and
typing.get_args() on an enum returns (). The choices list is therefore
EMPTY, and ansible rejects every possible value:

    value of device_model must be one of: , got: vedge-C8000V

Note the empty list before the comma. `vedge-C8000V` is a perfectly valid
member (DeviceModel.VEDGE_C8000V = 'vedge-C8000V'); the input was never
wrong, the introspection was.

MEASURED on the SD-WAN service lane, 2026-09-08. The merge had
just succeeded -- 142 base lines grafted to 163, interface
GigabitEthernet3 replaced, the device reachable -- and then:

    stage=service-template ... msg=value of device_model must be one of: ,
                                   got: vedge-C8000V
    stage=service-attach   ... msg=Template with name:
                                   skyforge-site1-edge01-service doesn't exist
    stage=service          ... verdict=NOT_PROVEN omp_seen=[]

The template was never created, so the attach could not find it.

All THREE call sites are fixed, not just the one that failed:
cli_templates (create), device_templates (attach -- it would have failed
on the very next step), and feature_templates. The helper accepts either
shape, so this keeps working whichever way upstream defines DeviceModel.

PROVEN both ways, by executing the helper against both shapes:
  Enum (what we ship):    ['vedge-C8000V', 'ISR4451'] -> accepted
  Literal (what upstream assumed): same -> accepted
  the shipped expression on that Enum: [] -> rejects everything

UPSTREAM ASK: FILE -- get_args() is the wrong introspection for an enum,
and any consumer on a catalystwan that defines DeviceModel this way has
every template module broken with a message that blames the caller's
input.
---
diff --git a/plugins/modules/cli_templates.py b/plugins/modules/cli_templates.py
index aff7e96..b86b704 100644
--- a/plugins/modules/cli_templates.py
+++ b/plugins/modules/cli_templates.py
@@ -122,6 +122,30 @@ except ImportError:
     from catalystwan.models.common import DeviceModel
 
 from ..module_utils.result import ModuleResult
+
+# DeviceModel IS AN ENUM IN THE catalystwan WE VENDOR, NOT A Literal.
+#
+# catalystwan 0.41.5.dev2 ships DeviceModel as enum.EnumType with 293
+# members. typing.get_args() on an Enum returns () -- it only yields the
+# arguments of a Literal/Union -- so `choices=list(get_args(DeviceModel))`
+# is an EMPTY list, and ansible then rejects every possible value:
+#
+#   value of device_model must be one of: , got: vedge-C8000V
+#
+# Note the empty list before the comma. `vedge-C8000V` is a perfectly valid
+# member value (DeviceModel.VEDGE_C8000V); nothing was wrong with the input.
+# Measured on the example lab's SD-WAN service lane 2026-09-08: the CLI
+# template was never created, so the attach then failed with "Template ...
+# does not exist" and every edge came back NOT_PROVEN.
+#
+# Accept both shapes so this keeps working whichever way upstream defines it.
+def _device_model_choices():
+    members = getattr(DeviceModel, "__members__", None)
+    if members:
+        return [m.value for m in DeviceModel]
+    return list(get_args(DeviceModel))
+
+
 from ..module_utils.vmanage_module import AnsibleCatalystwanModule
 
 State = Literal["present", "absent"]
@@ -143,7 +167,7 @@ def run_module():
         ),
         template_name=dict(type="str", required=True),
         template_description=dict(type="str", default=None),
-        device_model=dict(type="str", aliases=["device_type"], choices=list(get_args(DeviceModel)), default=None),
+        device_model=dict(type="str", aliases=["device_type"], choices=_device_model_choices(), default=None),
         config_file=dict(type="str", aliases=["running_config_file_path"]),
         force=dict(type="bool", default=False),
         timeout_seconds=dict(type="int", default=300),
